;;; A rule's `:assert` splits a name that starts with `and` — M1e2 **W22**,
;;; register row `W22/kw-and-in-assert` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked
;;; by S1e2.2.22 (2026-11-05) from the inventory of 2026-11-05's F1, on the
;;; user's ruling of that day (Q-M1e2.16).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `kw-neq-in-assert.ein` with the relation `andz` in place of `neqw`, the
;;; third word of the inventory of 2026-11-05's F1.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `00_ebnf.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `andz` is a name like any other. `r`
;;; concludes `(andz a)`: root holds `(andz a)` and `(p a)`, H is empty and R
;;; is {∅}, the answer of `kw-and-in-assert-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.22 the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read `(andz ?x)` as the
;;; conjunction `(and z ?x)`, whose first conjunct `z` is no fact, so the run
;;; stopped at `r`'s first firing: every command that saturated exited 1 with
;;; *rule 'r': its :assert concludes z, which is not a fact — …*. (`ein solve
;;; FILE` at `35186bc0`, release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the
;;; same.) Since then a keyword is a whole word, at the boundary `00_ebnf.md`
;;; § 1 states (the user's ruling of 2026-11-05 on Q-M1e2.17), and the program
;;; answers its twin's R.

(relation p Node) (relation andz Node)
(p a)
(rule r () :match (p ?x) :assert (andz ?x))
(query :goal (andz ?y))
